+function SetKindOfNode(n: Integer): TTypeKind;
+{ The set STORAGE CLASS an AST operand carries, for the ops that must agree on
+  a width. The node's own ASTTk first; an AN_IDENT whose ASTTk was never
+  stamped (the function-name result form leaves it 0) falls back to the
+  SYMBOL's kind, which is the same fallback the set-assignment arm already
+  carries for the same reason.
+
+  Answers tySet -- the wide class, the width everything was before tySmallSet
+  -- for anything it cannot identify. Guessing narrow would truncate a real
+  set; guessing wide only forgoes the saving. }
+begin
+  SetKindOfNode := tySet;
+  if n < 0 then Exit;
+  if IntToTypeKind(ASTTk[n]) = tySmallSet then
+    SetKindOfNode := tySmallSet
+  else if (ASTKind[n] = AN_IDENT) and (ASTIVal[n] >= 0) and (ASTIVal[n] < SymCount) and
+          (Syms[ASTIVal[n]].TypeKind = tySmallSet) and (not Syms[ASTIVal[n]].IsArray) then
+    SetKindOfNode := tySmallSet;
+end;
+
+function IRSetOperandWidth(n: Integer): Integer;
+{ Bytes of the set an ADDRESS node points at — what `x in s`, Include and
+  Exclude need in order to bound the element ordinal they index with.
+
+  The bound is not cosmetic. Every backend clamped the element to 0..255 so an
+  out-of-range one could not deref past a 32-byte set; against a FOUR-byte set
+  that same clamp reads (and, for Include, WRITES) up to 28 bytes past the
+  object. `var x: Integer; s: set of 0..7; x in s` with x = 100 is ordinary
+  code FPC accepts and answers False for, so the clamp has to follow the set.
+
+  IRTk first; an IR_LEA whose tk was never stamped falls back to the SYMBOL,
+  the same two-step SetKindOfNode makes on the AST side. Anything unidentified
+  answers 32 — the pre-tySmallSet width, which is the conservative direction
+  for a READ and is what every set was before. }
+begin
+  IRSetOperandWidth := 32;
+  if n < 0 then Exit;
+  if TypeIsSet(IntToTypeKind(IRTk[n])) then
+    IRSetOperandWidth := SetSlotBytes(IntToTypeKind(IRTk[n]))
+  else if (IRKind[n] = IR_LEA) and (IRA[n] >= 0) and (IRA[n] < SymCount) and
+          TypeIsSet(Syms[IRA[n]].TypeKind) then
+    IRSetOperandWidth := SetSlotBytes(Syms[IRA[n]].TypeKind);
+end;
+
+function IRSetWidth(node: Integer): Integer;
+{ Bytes of the SET operand of a set opcode — the ONE place a backend asks how
+  wide the bitmap in front of it is, so the answer cannot drift between seven
+  emitters the way a literal 32 did.
+
+  IRTk carries the set's storage kind on IR_SET_LIT / IR_SET_COPY / IR_SET_BINOP.
+  IR_SET_CMP is the exception and has to be: its IRTk is the RESULT type
+  (tyBoolean), so the operands' kind rides in IRIVal, which that opcode does not
+  otherwise use.
+
+  A node whose kind is neither set kind answers 32 — the width every set was
+  before tySmallSet existed. That is deliberate: a producer that forgets to
+  stamp the kind gets the OLD behaviour rather than a zero-byte copy, so the
+  failure is a missed optimisation and not a silently empty set.
+  bug-a-a-set-is-32-bytes-whatever-its-bounds-and-the-ir-opcode-says-so }
+begin
+  if IRKind[node] = IR_SET_CMP then
+    IRSetWidth := SetSlotBytes(IntToTypeKind(Integer(IRIVal[node])))
+  else
+    IRSetWidth := SetSlotBytes(IntToTypeKind(IRTk[node]));
+end;
